WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.850
All right, so there's a new
project that's under a year old

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:10.769
and we're integrating the long
term approach of Jane and Diane and data

00:00:10.769 --> 00:00:15.450
collection with some new technologies to
really try and get at, in various

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:19.470
ways, weaning and mountain gorillas and
chimpanzees - the two animals that are

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:24.449
most like us, that are most likely some
sort of representative for where we came

00:00:24.449 --> 00:00:29.010
from, okay? This is in partnership with
many, many people, but in the Jane Goodall

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:33.570
Institute, the Fossey Fund, Max Planck
Institute, my institution, Franklin and

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:37.559
Marshall College, and George Washington
University. And we've got cute baby apes

00:00:37.559 --> 00:00:44.579
there so that's always good. Okay, so the
first thing we aim to do in this project

00:00:44.579 --> 00:00:49.590
is to really well characterize those
dietary transitions from exclusively

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:55.289
feeding on mother's milk to that
transition to solid food. And we know

00:00:55.289 --> 00:01:00.210
that it's a process, not a hard
line. And the way we have done this up to

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:04.320
this point is by doing behavioral
observations by watching these apes for

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:07.740
years and years and years and
documenting when they're suckling at

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:12.689
their mother's breast and when they
start feeding on solid food. Now remember,

00:01:12.689 --> 00:01:16.979
I mentioned to you that weaning in apes
takes four to seven years. It's a really

00:01:16.979 --> 00:01:20.580
long period of time. And of course
they're eating solid food well before

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:24.540
seven years old. And so what we want to
do in this project is really narrow that

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:30.180
window, or understand that window much
better. And so in addition to this

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:34.200
behavioral approach of really analyzing
our long-term data for suckling behavior

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:40.310
and eating solid foods, what we're doing
is collecting a lot of ape poop, okay? Ape

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:46.710
poop is gold in my world. You can
find out so many things from ape poop. You

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:51.000
have no idea. How stressed they are, what
diseases they have, what they ate

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:56.670
yesterday. It's amazing. One of the newer
things you can find out from ape poop is

00:01:56.670 --> 00:02:01.409
if you analyze the stable isotopes in
that poop, you can actually figure out

00:02:01.409 --> 00:02:06.960
what trophic level an infant is relative
to its mother. So you can measure stable

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:13.379
isotopes of nitrogen and essentially if
the kid is enriched in nitrogen relative

00:02:13.379 --> 00:02:16.299
to the mother,
that means that that kid is still a

00:02:16.299 --> 00:02:22.360
trophic level above the mom, i.e., I'm still
eating mom; I'm still feeding primarily

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:26.860
on mom, right? And when the levels of
nitrogen come down to the same as mom,

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:31.000
you know that the contribution of
mother's milk to the diet has leveled

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.290
off, and they're eating, surviving
primarily on solid food. So this has been

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:40.930
done in one study in great apes by
Iulia Badescu. And this is, don't

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:43.959
worry about what the graph is saying all
that much, what's important is that

00:02:43.959 --> 00:02:48.849
horizontal line is infant age in months.
And anything above the line is when the

00:02:48.849 --> 00:02:52.930
kid is eating mom, and anything on the
line is when the kid is not. And what

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:57.700
this graph tells you is that around 25
to 30 months old, the kid looks just like

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:02.200
the mom. The kid is no longer eating mom
at that point. That's less than a

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:05.439
three-year-old. And remember, I told you
that weaning occurs between four and

00:03:05.439 --> 00:03:09.489
seven, so they're nutritionally
independent of mom well before they stop

00:03:09.489 --> 00:03:17.470
weaning. Which is kind of weird, right? So
we want to narrow that window a little

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:21.069
bit more, understand that a little bit
better, get more data from chimps and

00:03:21.069 --> 00:03:25.239
also from gorillas, because that type of
work has never been done in gorillas. The

00:03:25.239 --> 00:03:29.769
second thing we want to do is figure out
that huge window, four-to-seven years old,

00:03:29.769 --> 00:03:34.599
what are the sources of variation in
there? There's various theory that would

00:03:34.599 --> 00:03:39.819
suggest that that might change depending
on maternal age, whether mom is large

00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:44.199
body size, or in better body condition.
there's some theory that suggests that

00:03:44.199 --> 00:03:48.729
infants have to reach a particular size
themselves to be weaned, so infant size

00:03:48.729 --> 00:03:53.079
for age. Birth order, maybe firstborn kids
are different than second born kids.

00:03:53.079 --> 00:03:57.489
Maybe infant sex matters. We just have no
idea what could contribute to this big

00:03:57.489 --> 00:04:02.290
variation that we see. So a lot of these
variables we can understand from our

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:05.799
long-term records, right? Maternal age. We
know who these moms are, we know when

00:04:05.799 --> 00:04:09.340
they were born, we know how old they are.
But, and I should say, I say that like

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:11.829
it's really simple, it's only because
these studies have been going on more

00:04:11.829 --> 00:04:15.639
than 50 years, okay? So let me point that out there. That's why

00:04:15.639 --> 00:04:19.690
we know that. We know the birth order,
we know the infant sex, obviously, but we

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:23.919
don't have, or we didn't have until
recently, a good non-invasive way to

00:04:23.919 --> 00:04:29.500
study size in
animals. Attempts to measure a wild

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:36.040
chimpanzee or gorilla will fail if you
ask them to step on a scale or hold up a

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:38.800
ruler.
You might not get the ruler or

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:43.480
your arm back. So we've had to develop
ways that do this without

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:48.550
disturbing them. And what's come out of
some research, actually on horses, and

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:52.900
other mammals is this approach of a
parallel laser photogrammetry.

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:59.110
So essentially, you build this box here
on the left, which, that's the exact box I

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:03.940
just built in December, you try to get it
through airport security all the way to

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:13.630
Tanzania, which was surprisingly easy. And
what that does is, it's a series of, it's

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:17.260
a laser beam split by two prisms. And
they're perfectly parallel all the time.

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:20.560
And because they're perfectly parallel
all the time, they're always exactly 4

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:23.290
centimeters apart. So you take that to
the field,

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:27.880
you give your collaborators the box, they
put it on a camera, they point it at a

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:32.530
chimp, and now you have a scale. You're
projecting something exactly 4

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:37.240
centimeters apart onto a wild animal. You
can use that as a scale to measure body

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:41.650
size, okay? That's our little
scale blown up because those dots are

00:05:41.650 --> 00:05:46.360
pretty hard to see. This doesn't bother
them. These chimpanzees are used to weird

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:50.260
people pointing weird stuff at them all
the time because the studies been going

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:54.160
on 60 years. So this doesn't bother them.
So again, we're doing this in both

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:58.510
gorillas and chimpanzees to try and suss
out some of these sources of variation.

00:05:58.510 --> 00:06:09.920
New tech with long-term data.

